.TH HOSTNAME 1
.SH NAME
hostname \- print or set the name of this machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hostname
[\ name\ ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hostname
prints the name of this machine. Given a
.IR name ,
it makes that the machine's name; only root can. A name is 1 to 8
letters, digits and \-.
.PP
XENIX keeps the machine's name as the first line of /etc/systemid, the
file Micnet and UUCP read it from, and that is where hostname reads and
writes it. Any lines after the first are left as they are.
.PP
The C shell prompt that mkuser gives new accounts shows this name.
.SH FILES
.TP 16
/etc/systemid
the machine's name, on its first line
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is 0 when the name was printed or set, 1 when
/etc/systemid could not be read or written or the name is not a name,
and 2 after a mistake in the arguments.
.SH SEE ALSO
uname(1)
.SH HISTORY
A
.B hostname
command appeared in 4.2BSD.
.SH AUTHORS
This hostname was written for the xenix86-devsys system tools.
.SH BUGS
XENIX 2.1.3's kernel has a node name of its own, which
.B uname \-n
prints, but nothing sets it, so it stays empty whatever hostname is
given.
